  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warm and increasingly humid conditions through \nFriday, with the highest temperatures focused inland.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nThe overall 500mb ridging remains over the area with very warm\n850mb temperatures into Friday. The ridge becomes contaminated \nallowing for the return of clouds and showers but the airmass \nremains very warm. Although not record breaking it will be \nuncomfortable for some with minor to moderate heat risk mainly \nimpacting the most vulnerable groups. The focus of the hot \ntemperatures shifts north into Northern Maine where highs will \ntop out in the upper 80s to around 90F for the Central Highlands\nnorthward to St. John Valley. Expecting mid to upper 70s \nDowneast thanks to a wind off the Gulf of Maine and Bay of Fundy\nwith low to mid 80s around Bangor to Topsfield. A touch of the \nmuggies today with dew points climbing into the low to mid 60s \nso it will feel humid. Friday the 925-850mb airmass is still\nwarm but significant cloud cover, showers and SE winds off the\nBay of Fundy will result in a little bit lower highs. Low to mid\n80s for Northern Maine, Central Highlands to Bangor Region with\n60s-70s in the Downeast with the coldest temps near the\nshoreline. It will be pretty muggy on Friday with dew points in\nthe 63-68F range for most areas with some 70F readings in the\nBangor region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers this evening and persist into the \nweekend. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nShortwave moves over the region today, bringing some moisture \nand instability into the region. Decent low-level lapse rates, \nbut southeast flow provides a marine layer over eastern Maine, \nstabilizing the surface. There is some mid-level instability, \nbut mid-level lapse rates are very poor. Without strong forcing,\nnot expecting too much in terms of storm development. Could be \na few thunderstorms in the west, such as northern Somerset and \nwestern Piscataquis, since the marine layer will be confined to \nthe windward side of the mountains. \n\nOn Friday, weak ridging moves in over the region as a cold front\ncreeps in from the west, with some increasing instability. \nChance for some showers and an isolated thunderstorm to \ndevelop, mainly confined to the western regions. Stable\nsoutheast flow over Downeast should keep should showers north \nof Bangor. \n\nFriday night into Saturday, cold front moves closer in from the\nwest. Could be some thunderstorms that stay off to the west\novernight. Diurnal heating decreases, but forcing from the front\ncould help develop an isolated storm and continued showers. \nDuring the day, showers and isolated storms could move over \nNortheastern Aroostook, diminishing by nighttime.",
